Security re-review of #12, rules narrowed as requested:
- Raw notrack covers only:
  - TCP on own ephemeral ports (sport out, dport in),
  - UDP on those ports with the VPC CIDRs,
  - the peer's UDP to 3780,
  - UDP with 169.254.169.123:123 and 169.254.169.253:53.
- The canary range 61000 to 65535 is outside the ephemeral range, so it stays tracked without a special rule.
- INPUT stateless accepts:
  - non-SYN TCP to the own address on 32768:60999 only,
  - UDP from exactly those two link-local services and ports,
  - four ICMP error types to the own address, at 50/s with a burst of 100.
- Non-SYN TCP to any other port, including the canary range, still hits the REJECT reset rule, as before #12. A privileged test sends crafted ACKs and a SYN from outside the VPC and checks the rule counters for each path, 200 runs stable.
- ICMP threat: Linux matches errors to sockets by 4-tuple and checks TCP sequence numbers. Path MTU can't drop below min_pmtu 552. Unreachable messages are soft errors on established TCP. They can abort a matching handshake or DNS lookup, which is retried. The rate limit bounds it.
- Residual: UDP from VPC hosts to the NAT's ephemeral ports is accepted. VPC traffic already reached every port before.
- Checks: unit tests on exact rules, the full 17-case campaign, golangci-lint, go test.
